\label{lemma:recurrent}
        If $X$ is a 
        $\Gamma$-flow with no wandering points, then for every nonempty open set $U \subseteq X$, there exists a point $x \in U$ such that the set $\mathsf{Vis}(x,U)$ is infinite.

\label{lemma:one_set}
        Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a family of finite subsets of $\Gamma$ and let $X$ be an $\mathcal{F}$-minimal 
        $\Gamma$-flow. If $A \subseteq X$ is a non-meager Baire-measurable set, then for some $F  \in \mathcal{F}$, $F^{-1} \cdot A$ is comeager in $X$.

\label{lemma:many_sets}
        Let $\mathcal{F} = \set{F_n}_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ be a family of finite subsets of $\Gamma$ and let $X$ be an $\mathcal{F}$-minimal 
        $\Gamma$-flow. If $(A_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is a sequence of non-meager Baire-measurable subsets of $X$, then the set $\bigcup_{n \in \mathbb{N}} (F_n \cdot A_n)$ is comeager in $X$.
